STYLE - how this repo is kept

Each rule in the first part is here because something went wrong without it. A rule with no failure behind it is in
the second part, and is only a habit.

things that went wrong, and the rule that came out of each

1. Numbers in text went stale. The size changed six times (247, 224, 217, 214, 210, 209) and the source header kept
   describing a design it no longer had.
   -> The byte count is written in README and nowhere else; `make` prints it. Comments in microtris.asm say why an
      instruction is there or what trick it is, never what it does and never how big the file is.

2. The first six checks passed while the program wrote 64 KB over video memory every frame, and a vertical I could
   vanish at a wall. Only later checks saw it.
   -> A check is added when a bug got past the existing ones, not before. It must be shown to fail on a program that
      has the bug (`make holder-bug`), or it proves nothing.

3. Numbers in README came from throwaway scripts that were not in the repo.
   -> A measurable claim in README or CLAIM names the make target that produces it. No target, no claim.

4. Third-party code was committed without a licence, and the history had to be rewritten.
   -> Nothing goes in the repo that could not be published. Other people's files are fetched by make and checked
      against a sha256 (build/tinytris.com).

5. Compiled python files were committed, and the history had to be rewritten again.
   -> Build output and caches are never committed. Look at `git status` before every commit.

6. A full test run took 29 minutes once, on a loaded machine, so it stopped being run.
   -> `make quick` while editing. `make test` before every commit that touches microtris.asm or test/. `make matrix`
      and `make dosbox` before anything is published.

7. Work that was not the owner's to do alone nearly went out: a push, a message to someone, a submission.
   -> Anything that leaves this machine waits for the owner to say so, each time. History is only rewritten before
      the first push, and a bundle of the old history is kept outside the repo.

8. The program is derived from someone else's, and the numbers saying how much were first wrong (112, then 104).
   -> README says what is taken from where and how it was counted. If the program or any text was written with a
      tool's help, README says that too.

habits (no failure behind them, this is just how it is written)

- Documents are plain text, named in capitals without an extension: README, CLAIM, STYLE, LICENSE.
- The program is at the top. Everything that only checks it is under test/.
- Assembly: labels in column 0, instructions at column 8, mnemonics and hex in lowercase, `.name` for labels that
  belong to one routine, at most 120 columns.
- Python: 4 spaces, single quotes, at most 120 columns, run as `python3 -I`. Outside the standard library only unicorn
  and capstone, plus Pillow for the optional DOSBox check. No formatter or linter is configured.
- Makefile: POSIX make, no extensions.
- Commit messages: one line, lowercase, what changed. A size change says the number ("209 bytes").
